\subtree{
\title{S-F5: canonical free variables + pair table + eager reduction (v0)}

\p{The official \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} is not a function of the pair alone. (a) It has a mode: \code{\verb~|infer_app~} sets \code{\verb~|m_eager_reduce~} while it compares the type of an argument of the form \code{\verb~|eagerReduce _ _~} with its domain; under it \code{\verb~|lazy_delta_reduction~} folds Nat operations although a free variable is present. (b) Its caches (\code{\verb~|m_success~}, the WHNF caches) live for one declaration and are keyed by terms whose free variables are unique per binder instance.}

\p{With \code{\verb~|G x := And.rec (fun _ _ => 0) x~} (regular), \code{\verb~|X := Nat.add (G (And.intro ..)) 0~}, \code{\verb~|Y p := Nat.add (G p) 0~}:}

\ul{
\li{plain mode, \code{\verb~|p~} a free variable: no fold; \code{\verb~|Nat.add~} and \code{\verb~|G~} compare their arguments; \code{\verb~|And.intro ..~} against \code{\verb~|p~} is proof irrelevance: \code{\verb~|X = Y p~} is \strong{T};}
\li{eager mode: \code{\verb~|X~} folds to \code{\verb~|0~} first; \code{\verb~|0~} against \code{\verb~|Nat.add (G p) 0~} unfolds to \code{\verb~|0~} against the stuck \code{\verb~|And.rec .. p~}: \strong{F} (confirmed with the official checker: \code{\verb~|work/soundness/probe/h2~}, \code{\verb~|h3~} reject, \code{\verb~|h4~} accepts).}
}

\p{\code{\verb~|canon-fvar-pos-across-binders-eager-bad~} is ONE theorem \code{\verb~|And.intro (fun p => Eq.refl X) (fun p => (fun h => h) (eagerReduce (X = X) (Eq.refl X))) : (forall p, X = Y p) /\ (forall p, X = Y p)~}. Officially the second lambda has a new free variable, the success cache has no entry for it, the eager comparison fails: REJECT. In the machine both binders are \code{\verb~|FVAR(And True True, 0)~}, the pair has POS from the first scope, and \code{\verb~|Conv.pairHit~} answers POS also while ER is set: ACCEPT, in v0. \code{\verb~|krule-memo-across-binders-eager-bad~} is the same through the K rule (the indices of an opaque proof of \code{\verb~|X = Y p~}; the recursor reduces in the second scope where the official kernel leaves it stuck). The twins \code{\verb~|canon-fvar-pos-across-binders-plain-ok~}, \code{\verb~|warm-pos-same-declaration-eager-ok~} (plain first, then eager in ONE scope: the official success cache answers, ACCEPT) and \code{\verb~|...-eager-first-bad~} are judged correctly by v0.}

\p{Soundness condition that is broken: "a POS pair (and any memo whose value depends on a comparison) answers a query only if the official kernel would answer T for it in the current mode and history". It holds for plain mode (the official plain procedure is a function of the pair), it does not hold across the mode switch.}

\p{M6 adopted fix (1) below at 15:07Z in its dynamic form: \code{\verb~|Machine.enterER()~} marks the declaration and \code{\verb~|MachineSession.check~} answers DECLINE for it in every regime; the reference engine decides (statistics \code{\verb~|machine.eagerDeclines~}). The 11 exports of the suite that enter eager reduction carry the note \code{\verb~|eager-decline~}. Taking these declarations back to the machine is fix (2), not started.}

\p{Fixes as proposed (sent 14:38Z and 15:06Z): eager reduction is the only mode switch of the official kernel and occurs in 164 of Init's 55,401 and 203 of Std's 99,235 def / thm / opaque / axiom declarations (0.30 \% / 0.20 \%, counted on the exports). (1) Exact and cheapest: at \code{\verb~|Machine.enterER()~}, if this declaration has already compared anything in plain mode, give up with a DECLINE (the machine cannot know whether the official success cache holds the pair: same scope yes, other scope no); the reference engine decides. In practice nearly every declaration that applies \code{\verb~|eagerReduce~} is then declined. (2) Keep such declarations on the machine: canonical variables off for them (every OPEN a fresh identity), or stamps on the pair entries and on every comparison-dependent memo (WHNF / CORE words that went through the K rule) and a flush at the mode switch. (3) Not sufficient: "do not read POS while ER is set" alone (the K rule's memo), and a fresh session (the two scopes are in one declaration). The order "eager first, plain later" needs no rule for soundness: a T computed under eager reduction from an empty history is also T in plain mode (folding anticipates what unfolding computes; the K rule fires in fewer cases).}
}

\subtree{
\title{S-F4: POS pairs survive a declaration (\code{\verb~|warm~}; fixed)}

\p{The same pair in two declarations: \code{\verb~|S.a : forall p, X = Y p := fun p => Eq.refl X~} (plain), then \code{\verb~|S.bad~} needs it under \code{\verb~|eagerReduce~}. v0 rejected (fresh session), every set with \code{\verb~|warm~} accepted (jar \code{\verb~|608b84b2b142~}). \code{\verb~|krule-eager-alone-bad~} shows that the declaration that seeds the table need not look related (the opaque proof \code{\verb~|e~} itself seeds it). M6's fix of 14:46Z (\code{\verb~|Machine.enterER~}, \code{\verb~|erTainted~}, \code{\verb~|erSeen~}): a declaration that enters eager reduction while the session holds earlier state is checked again in a fresh session, and nothing it derived is seen by a later declaration. (First seen from another side by M5 at 14:25Z: \code{\verb~|memocheck~} with \code{\verb~|warm~} on \code{\verb~|bad/bugs/rec-of-subst-prop~}, a POS pair of an earlier declaration that the re-computation under eager reduction does not reproduce.)}
}

\subtree{
\title{S-F1 / S-F2: congruence shortcuts where the official procedure is not a congruence (fixed)}

\p{B1 (same-head probe: recursors, quotient eliminators, abbrevs, theorems), B12 (closure congruence), B3 (skipped proof positions), B4 (skipped unread positions) and the Prop mask of structure eta answered T from "the arguments / slots are pairwise convertible". The official \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} reduces before it compares: \code{\verb~|And.rec f (And.intro ..)~} becomes \code{\verb~|5~}, \code{\verb~|And.rec f h~} (h an axiom or variable) stays stuck (no structure eta for a proposition), and \code{\verb~|5~} against a stuck recursor is false - although \code{\verb~|And.intro .. = h~} by proof irrelevance. The official kernel compares arguments first only for a same-head definition with a REGULAR hint (\code{\verb~|probe-b1-def-proof-arg-ok~} is accepted officially, \code{\verb~|probe-b1-abbrev-proof-arg~} and \code{\verb~|probe-b1-theorem-head~} are rejected). It is also not transitive (\code{\verb~|nontransitive-*~}: \code{\verb~|d intro = d h~} and \code{\verb~|d intro = 5~} are accepted, \code{\verb~|d h = 5~} is rejected; the machine does not close them). M4 removed B1, B12, B2, B3, B4 and the ETAS mask from every switch set at 14:30Z (system property \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.m4=congruence,skipargs~} for measurements only); \code{\verb~|M4OfficialTest~} and the rows of package S guard it.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. Method and tools (all kept)}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|work/soundness/tools/lk.py~}: builds exports on top of \code{\verb~|init-prelude~} (imports the dependency cone of the constants a test names, structural interning as the exporter does, a recursor generator for non-mutual inductive types that the official checker accepts for seven shapes). \code{\verb~|gen/batch1..8.py~}: 349 hostile exports.}
\li{\code{\verb~|tools/judge.py~}: the official verdict, the reference engine and the machine through the launcher under up to 17 switch sets (suffixes: \code{\verb~|@1~} = \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.elabAt=1~}, \code{\verb~|!d0~} = host depth 0, \code{\verb~|#t4~} = four workers); flags WRONG-ACCEPT / WRONG-REJECT / M-INCOMPLETE / M-DECLINE / REF-DIFFERS.}
\li{\code{\verb~|tools/mutate.py~}: seven mutation kinds that edit ONE OCCURRENCE inside ONE declaration (the path from the root is copied, so a shared sub-term keeps its meaning elsewhere): \code{\verb~|LEVEL_ARG~}, \code{\verb~|SWAP_UNIV_PARAMS~}, \code{\verb~|SIBLING_ARG~} (the argument of another application of the same function), \code{\verb~|LIT_SUCC_EQ~} / \code{\verb~|LIT_SUCC_NE~}, \code{\verb~|BETA_WRAP~}, \code{\verb~|LET_WRAP~}. The existing kinds of \code{\verb~|rlvm-testkit~} edit records in place; the request to adopt these is in \code{\verb~|rlvm-testkit.log~}.}
\li{Permanent regression: \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine/src/test/resources/s/~} (329 exports, gzip, manifest with the official verdict and the attack; 45 accepted, 284 rejected by the official checker), \code{\verb~|SHostileTest~} (fast group, at every publication of every package: v0 and \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} on every export, the staged switches on their targets), \code{\verb~|SHostileSlowTest~} (17 switch sets x every export; strict \code{\verb~|noKnownFindingIsLeft~}). The rows of the four fixed findings are strict for everybody; the two rows of S-F3 are marked \code{\verb~|levels~} and printed until it is fixed.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. Shortcuts and memos reviewed}

\p{Condition = what must hold for the shortcut to give the official verdict. Evidence = the exports of \code{\verb~|manifest.tsv~} by prefix (all judged against the official checker under the switch sets of section 5).}

